Configuration options
Use Key = Value, # comments and ["quoted", "strings"] lists.
Defaults keep line breaks; Layout = Canonical reflows code.
Protected code stays unchanged. Invalid keys or values are errors.
Use --generate-config for all options, values and defaults.
Use --generate-config google (or ytsaurus/llvm) for a preset.

Inheritance
include = "base.hit-fmt" loads a base config.
- Paths start beside this config; @/ starts at the nearest ancestor
  with a .git or .arc marker. Absolute paths work too.
- Local settings win. Lists replace inherited lists; [] clears them.
- Validate after merging. Include depth: 64 files maximum.
  Cycles, missing files and duplicate includes are errors.

    include = "@/configs/base.hit-fmt"
    ColumnLimit = 100

Layout and width
- Layout: Preserve keeps line breaks; Canonical reflows code to fit.
  Category overrides accept Inherit, Preserve or Canonical.
- Break: IfNeeded allows fitting code to flatten; Always requests a
  break even when it fits. Empty, indivisible or protected syntax may
  prevent it; a nested break alone may not satisfy Always.
- ColumnLimit: 0 disables width wrapping and diagnostics; 1..10000
  sets the limit. Required breaks still apply at 0.
- WidthDiagnostics: Off or Report. Counts Unicode code points, not
  display cells; invalid UTF-8 bytes count individually. Tabs expand
  to tab stops when checking output widths.

Canonical layout
    Layout = Canonical
    IndentWidth = 2
    ColumnLimit = 80
    WidthDiagnostics = Report
    BaseListsLayout = Preserve

Lists and alignment
- Categories override shared rules: CallsPacking, ParametersBreak, etc.
  Inherit uses shared values. Base/constructor lists have no Closer.
- Packing: Fill packs items; VerticalWhenBroken puts each item on a line
  when the list breaks; AllOrVertical keeps a full row or splits every item.
- FirstItem: InlineIfFits or NextLine. Closer: SameLine or OwnLine.
  Both apply to broken lists, independently of item packing.
- Alignment: Delimiter aligns after the opener, with a fallback when
  the next item and suffix cannot fit. Block indents from the block.
  Zero offsets use IndentWidth.
- TemplateParameters includes generic lambdas; TemplateArguments includes
  named casts. Shared >> closers stay together.
- KeepMultiline = true prevents fitting authored multiline constructs from
  becoming a single line. Canonical break positions and indentation still
  apply; inline constructs may wrap. Explicit brace placement and protected
  or Preserve layout retain their authority. Default false permits joining.
- StringLiteralFragmentsAsArguments = true makes adjacent literals use
  Calls layout, break policy, packing and indentation as one expression.
  Direct arguments share their call's broken shape. Default false keeps
  Expressions formatting and authored layout follows those existing rules.
- CallsSoleArgumentFirstItem overrides CallsFirstItem for a sole argument.
  With StringLiteralFragmentsAsArguments, fragmented strings instead use
  CallsFirstItem.
- CallsSoleLambdaArgumentInline = true attaches a direct sole lambda to
  the call opener and closer: Call([] { ... });. Overrides CallsBreak,
  CallsFirstItem, CallsSoleArgumentFirstItem and CallsCloser in canonical
  calls. Lambda header/body rules and protected comments still apply.
  KeepMultiline does not split these attached call boundaries.
  Default false uses ordinary call rules.
- IfConditionsPacking = VerticalWhenBroken splits top-level &&/|| in
  wrapped if headers. Requires canonical header/expression layout;
  parentheses and other controls keep their own rules.
- Block comments after separators move with the next item. Other
  attachments stay intact; lambdas indent from their opening line.

List defaults
    ListBreak = IfNeeded
    ListPacking = Fill
    ListFirstItem = InlineIfFits
    ListCloser = SameLine
    ListAlignment = Delimiter
    ContinuationOffset = 4
    AlignmentFallbackOffset = 4

One argument per line
    CallsBreak = Always
    CallsPacking = VerticalWhenBroken
    CallsFirstItem = NextLine
    CallsCloser = OwnLine
    CallsAlignment = Block
    # Let a sole argument start inline if it fits.
    CallsSoleArgumentFirstItem = InlineIfFits
    # Attach sole lambdas even when ordinary calls use an own-line closer.
    CallsSoleLambdaArgumentInline = true

Keep argument rows together
    ColumnLimit = 32
    CallsPacking = AllOrVertical
    CallsFirstItem = NextLine
    CallsCloser = OwnLine
    # ProcessPendingTasks(
    #     first, second
    # );

Template lists
    TemplateParametersBreak = IfNeeded
    TemplateParametersPacking = VerticalWhenBroken
    TemplateParametersFirstItem = NextLine
    TemplateParametersCloser = SameLine
    TemplateParametersAlignment = Block
    TemplateArgumentsBreak = IfNeeded
    TemplateArgumentsPacking = VerticalWhenBroken
    TemplateArgumentsFirstItem = NextLine
    TemplateArgumentsCloser = SameLine
    TemplateArgumentsAlignment = Block

Wrapped if conditions
    Layout = Canonical
    IfConditionsPacking = VerticalWhenBroken
    ControlHeadersFirstItem = InlineIfFits
    BinaryOperatorSide = Leading

Expressions and chains
- ExpressionsBreak and ChainsBreak default to IfNeeded.
  Wrapping respects precedence. OperatorSide options choose Leading
  or Trailing. ExpressionsAlignment = Block shares the enclosing
  continuation; Operand aligns to the first operand. Indented adds
  ContinuationOffset from the first operand line's indentation, even
  inside a continued call argument. Assignments keep block continuation
  indentation; ternary delimiters follow TernaryOperatorIndentation.
- AssignmentsPacking inherits ExpressionsPacking. Fill can keep an RHS
  prefix inline; VerticalWhenBroken splits assignment boundaries.
  AssignmentRhsPlacement = NextLine puts initializers below the operator
  in canonical expressions. Always breaks still apply.
  With StringLiteralFragmentsAsArguments, broken string initializers also
  start their first fragment below the assignment operator.
- TernaryLayout = ThreePart breaks ? and : together when wrapping;
  Flexible may split them independently. TernaryOperatorIndentation
  = Indent adds a level when the condition starts on a continued line;
  Align shares the continuation.
- Chains include . / -> members, factory()(arg), and subscript suffixes.
  Nested arguments and index expressions keep their own layout policies.
  ChainsLayout controls breaks before [, while ExpressionsLayout controls
  bracket interiors. For example, data["group"]["settings"] can wrap before
  ["settings"] with the configured chain continuation indentation.
  ChainsBreak = Always needs multiple calls after the receiver;
  fields, subscripts and single calls wrap as needed. Control headers prefer
  other breaks; nested lambdas keep ordinary chain rules.
- AllOrVertical keeps a chain tail together or splits all its links.
  For expressions and IfConditionsPacking it means VerticalWhenBroken.

Expression wrapping
    ExpressionsLayout = Canonical
    ExpressionsBreak = IfNeeded
    ExpressionsPacking = Fill
    ExpressionsAlignment = Block
    BinaryOperatorSide = Trailing
    ShiftOperatorSide = Leading
    AssignmentOperatorSide = Trailing
    AssignmentRhsPlacement = InlineIfFits
    TernaryOperatorSide = Leading
    TernaryLayout = ThreePart
    TernaryOperatorIndentation = Align

Indent a nested expression
    ExpressionsAlignment = Indented
    ContinuationOffset = 4
    # CheckEqual(
    #     actual,
    #     prefix +
    #         converted +
    #         suffix
    # );

Keep assignment prefixes inline
    ExpressionsPacking = VerticalWhenBroken
    AssignmentsPacking = Fill
    AssignmentRhsPlacement = InlineIfFits
    # When the RHS wraps:
    # auto ready = firstCheck() ||
    #     secondCheck();

Call chains
    ChainsLayout = Canonical
    ChainsBreak = IfNeeded
    ChainsPacking = Fill
    ChainsAlignment = Block

Keep a chain tail together
    ColumnLimit = 40
    ChainsPacking = AllOrVertical
    # auto client = MakeServiceClient()
    #     .Configure(mode).Start();

Braces and bodies
- Brace placements: Attached, NextLine, NextLineIfHeaderBroken,
  NextLineIfHeaderIndented. The last breaks when the final output header
  line reaches body indentation; a closer at brace indentation attaches.
  Constructors/destructors may Inherit FunctionBracePlacement.
  CaseBlockBracePlacement applies to a case containing only one block.
- DeclarationHeadersLayout controls headers; BodiesLayout controls bodies.
  BodyShape and EmptyBodyShape default to Expanded. CompactIfFits allows
  inline bodies; CompactSingleStatementIfFits allows one simple statement
  within ColumnLimit, including a local declaration. Canonical bodies only.
- FunctionBodyShape and ControlBodyShape override nonempty function and
  control bodies independently; Inherit (default) uses BodyShape.
  Functions include constructors, destructors and configured body macros.
  Controls include braced/unbraced arms, try/catch and case blocks.
  Standalone blocks, records and namespaces keep BodyShape.
- Nested controls/blocks, records, namespaces and sibling comments or
  directives prevent single-statement compaction. Comment-only bodies
  are not empty; empty bodies use EmptyBodyShape. The single-statement
  mode expands empty bodies. Enums, lists and requirements use their own rules.
- RequiresExpressionBodyShape overrides requires-expression bodies, including
  compound and nested requirements. Inherit (default) uses BodyShape,
  independently of function, control and lambda overrides. Expanded puts
  requirements on separate lines; CompactIfFits keeps the whole body inline
  within ColumnLimit. BodiesLayout and KeepMultiline still apply.
  CompactSingleStatementIfFits expands requirement bodies. Requires clauses
  use RequiresClausePosition instead.
- LambdaBodiesLayout, LambdaBodyShape and LambdaEmptyBodyShape can
  override body policies independently. LambdaBodyShape inherits BodyShape,
  independently of FunctionBodyShape and ControlBodyShape.
- IndentNamespaces: Preserve, Indent or NoIndent (enclosing indent).
  NamespaceIndentWidth = 0 uses IndentWidth.
- IndentLinkageBlocks: Preserve, Indent or NoIndent (enclosing indent)
  inside braced extern "C" / extern "C++" blocks. LinkageIndentWidth = 0
  uses IndentWidth. Body line breaks are preserved; single extern
  declarations without braces are unaffected.
- Enumerators uses list policies; EnumBracePlacement follows the header.
  Base/constructor lists use Leading/Trailing ColonSide and CommaSide.
  InitializersTrailingComma: Preserve, Never or WhenMultiline, where legal.
  Trailing commas and closer placement are independent.

Braces and body shapes
    FunctionBracePlacement = NextLine
    ConstructorBracePlacement = Inherit
    DestructorBracePlacement = Inherit
    ControlBracePlacement = NextLineIfHeaderBroken
    CaseBlockBracePlacement = Attached
    BodiesLayout = Canonical
    BodyShape = Expanded
    FunctionBodyShape = CompactSingleStatementIfFits
    ControlBodyShape = Expanded
    RequiresExpressionBodyShape = CompactIfFits
    EmptyBodyShape = CompactIfFits
    LambdaBodiesLayout = Inherit
    LambdaBodyShape = CompactIfFits
    LambdaEmptyBodyShape = Inherit

Braces after continued headers
    FunctionBracePlacement = NextLineIfHeaderIndented
    ConstructorBracePlacement = Inherit
    # With canonical headers, keep ) { at the function indentation:
    # void Process(
    #     Request request
    # ) {
    # Move { below a header ending at or beyond the body indentation:
    # Worker::Worker() :
    #     Cache_(CreateCache())
    # {

Compact one-statement bodies
    BodiesLayout = Canonical
    FunctionBodyShape = CompactSingleStatementIfFits
    ControlBodyShape = Expanded
    FunctionBracePlacement = Attached
    # int Next(int x) { return x + 1; }
    # void Reset() { Clear(); Notify(); } stays expanded.

Namespaces, linkage, enums and initializers
    IndentNamespaces = NoIndent
    NamespaceIndentWidth = 0
    IndentLinkageBlocks = Indent
    LinkageIndentWidth = 0
    # extern "C" {
    #     int Entry();
    # }
    EnumBracePlacement = NextLineIfHeaderBroken
    EnumeratorsLayout = Canonical
    EnumeratorsBreak = Always
    EnumeratorsPacking = VerticalWhenBroken
    EnumeratorsCloser = OwnLine
    InitializersCloser = OwnLine
    InitializersTrailingComma = WhenMultiline

Blank lines
- Counts accept Preserve, 0..10000 or Minimum:N (N in 0..10000).
  Minimum:N retains larger authored gaps subject to caps.
  Positive counts force a break; zero adds no break.
  Existing blank lines stay unless overridden.
- NamespaceBlankLines* and LinkageBlankLines* apply inside nonempty
  blocks, outside attached comments.
- AccessSectionBlankLinesBefore separates access sections, including
  unlabeled initial members. It skips a label at the start of a record;
  AfterLabel needs following content.
- DeclarationBlankLines separates declarations. DefinitionBlankLines wins
  beside function, record or enum definitions, not lambdas/namespaces.
  DeclarationCategoryBlankLines overrides both between types, functions
  and variables; aliases are types, templates follow their entity.
- MemberDeclarationBlankLines and MemberDefinitionBlankLines override
  general counts; Inherit follows them.
- CaseBlankLines separates a case with statements from the next
  case/default label, including attributed labels. Empty stacked labels
  stay together. The gap follows the whole body, including [[fallthrough]];,
  and precedes attached label comments. Directives and unparsed syntax
  stop grouping. Preserve retains gaps; 0 removes empty lines; 1 adds one.
- BlockBlankLinesAfter separates a brace-terminated block/control statement
  from its next sibling, also after braced do/while. It skips body edges,
  else/catch continuations and initializer braces. Also applies after
  definitions, namespaces and linkage blocks; declaration counts win.
- ControlStatementBlankLinesBefore separates sibling if/for/while statements
  from preceding code, including declarations. It skips body starts and
  labels; optional semantic grouping with initializations is not inferred.
- MacroBlankLinesAround separates complete standalone calls selected by
  BlankLineMacroNames (unqualified identifiers or prefixes ending in *).
  Consecutive calls of the same name form a group with zero blank lines.
  Fluent suffixes stay inside their statement. Nested calls and body edges
  do not get gaps.
- MaxEmptyLines caps editable blank lines, not inline gaps or tokens.
  MemberGroupMaxEmptyLines overrides it between adjacent fields/methods or
  two method definitions, preserving authored group boundaries. Preserve
  uses the global cap. Semantic grouping remains the author's decision.
  Exact/minimum counts above their configured caps are invalid after
  inheritance. Exact comment/ruler counts override member-group caps.
  Comment attachments, directives and protection can stop these edits.

Structural blank lines
    NamespaceBlankLinesAfterOpen = 1
    NamespaceBlankLinesBeforeClose = 1
    LinkageBlankLinesAfterOpen = 1
    LinkageBlankLinesBeforeClose = 1
    AccessSectionBlankLinesBefore = 1
    AccessSectionBlankLinesAfterLabel = 1
    DeclarationBlankLines = 0
    DeclarationCategoryBlankLines = 1
    MemberDeclarationBlankLines = Preserve
    DefinitionBlankLines = 1
    MemberDefinitionBlankLines = Minimum:1
    CaseBlankLines = 1
    BlockBlankLinesAfter = 1
    ControlStatementBlankLinesBefore = 1
    MacroBlankLinesAround = 1
    BlankLineMacroNames = ["CHECK", "LOG_*"]
    MaxEmptyLines = 1
    MemberGroupMaxEmptyLines = 2

Spacing
- Values: Preserve, Space, NoSpace, GeneratedSpace, GeneratedNoSpace.
  Generated* applies when canonical layout rebuilds the owning syntax;
  nested edits alone do not activate it. Syntax safety always wins.
- DeclarationSpecifierSpacing controls consecutive word specifiers in
  declarations and types, such as static const int. Balanced decltype,
  alignas and extension operands retain their own rules.
  DeclarationNameSpacing controls the type-to-name boundary of a parsed
  declaration or parameter, including Box<int> value. Pointer binding,
  packs and nested declarators retain their own rules.
  Both default to GeneratedSpace. Preserve retains horizontal spaces/tabs
  even with canonical layout; Space also applies with preserved layout.
  NoSpace still keeps a separator where tokens would otherwise merge.
  Comments and protected/opaque syntax stay intact; layout controls breaks.
- Before/After controls each side separately.
  TemplateTypeArgumentInteriorSpacing = NoSpace permits nested >>;
  ScopeResolutionSpacing excludes leading ::. BraceInitializerSpacing
  controls the gap before {, except after comments.
- ArgumentCommentSpacing controls attached block comments in arguments
  and initializers. ArgumentNameCommentSpacing separately controls the
  gap after a leading /*name*/ or /*name=*/ label. Spaces and tabs
  around the name and optional = are accepted. Any single identifier
  is a label. Gaps between comments and before trailing comments use
  ArgumentCommentSpacing. Both default to Preserve. Newlines,
  contents, separator-facing gaps and line comments stay intact.
  Generated* needs canonical layout of that list.
- FunctionCvRefQualifierSpacing controls const/volatile to &/&& in
  function qualifiers. Inherit follows FunctionRefQualifierSpacing;
  Preserve keeps source spacing.
- AttributeSpaceBeforeSpecifier and AttributeSpaceAfterSpecifier control
  [[...]], __attribute__ and __declspec boundaries, not extension contents.
  AttributeInteriorSpacing covers C++ [[...]] only.

Additional syntax boundaries
- ReturnValueSpacing: Space between return and its ordinary operand.
  Example: return value; Default: Preserve.
- CoReturnValueSpacing: Space between co_return and its ordinary operand.
  Example: co_return value; Default: Preserve.
- ThrowOperandSpacing: Space between throw and its ordinary operand.
  Example: throw value; Default: Preserve.
- CoYieldValueSpacing: Space between co_yield and its operand.
  Example: co_yield value; Default: Preserve.
- CoAwaitOperandSpacing: Space between co_await and its ordinary operand.
  CoAwaitSpaceBeforeParentheses controls parenthesized operands.
  Example: co_await task; Default: Preserve.
- GotoLabelSpacing: Space between goto and its label.
  Example: goto done; Default: Preserve.
- CaseValueSpacing: Space between case and its expression.
  Example: case 1: Default: Preserve.
- NewTypeSpacing: Space before the type of a new-expression.
  Example: new Widget; Default: Preserve.
- DeleteOperandSpacing: Space before a delete operand; array-specific rules
  win.
  Example: delete pointer; Default: Preserve.
- BaseSpecifierSpacing: Spaces between base specifiers and the complete base
  type.
  Example: public virtual Base Default: Preserve.
- PackExpansionSpaceBeforeEllipsis: Space before a confirmed pack ellipsis,
  including sizeof....
  Example: Types... Default: GeneratedNoSpace.
- PackExpansionSpaceAfterEllipsis: Space after a confirmed pack ellipsis;
  specific rules win.
  Example: Types... values Default: GeneratedSpace.
- TemplateParameterNameSpacing: Space before the name of a type template
  parameter.
  Example: class Element Default: Preserve.
- TemplateHeaderSpacing: Space after a complete template header; layout
  controls breaks.
  Example: template<class T> void F(); Default: GeneratedSpace.
- RecordNameSpacing: Space between class/struct/union and its name.
  Example: struct Widget Default: Preserve.
- EnumKeywordSpacing: Space between enum and class/struct.
  Example: enum class Flag Default: Preserve.
- EnumNameSpacing: Space before the enumeration name.
  Example: enum class Flag Default: Preserve.
- NamespaceNameSpacing: Space between namespace and its name.
  Example: namespace Detail Default: Preserve.
- UsingKeywordSpacing: Spaces after using and namespace/typename
  introducers.
  Example: using namespace Detail; Default: Preserve.
- ImportKeywordSpacing: Space after import, outside header-name contents.
  Example: import module_name; Default: Preserve.
- ModuleKeywordSpacing: Space after module, outside partition punctuation.
  Example: module module_name; Default: Preserve.
- ExportKeywordSpacing: Space between export and its declaration or opening
  brace.
  Example: export class Widget; Default: Preserve.
- LinkageLanguageSpacing: Space between extern and the language string.
  Example: extern "C" Default: Preserve.
- LinkageBraceSpacing: Space before a language-linkage opening brace.
  Example: extern "C" { Default: Preserve.
- LinkageBodyInteriorSpacing: Spaces inside language-linkage braces.
  Example: extern "C" { int F(); } Default: Preserve.
- ExportBodyInteriorSpacing: Spaces inside export-block braces.
  Example: export { int F(); } Default: Preserve.
- ModulePartitionColonSpaceBefore: Space before a module partition colon.
  Example: module_name:part Default: Preserve.
- ModulePartitionColonSpaceAfter: Space after a module partition colon.
  Example: module_name:part Default: Preserve.
- ConceptNameSpacing: Space between concept and its name.
  Example: concept Predicate = true; Default: Preserve.
- RequiresClauseSpaceBefore: Space before a parsed requires clause.
  Example: void F() requires Predicate; Default: Preserve.
- RequiresClauseSpaceAfter: Space after requires in clauses and nested
  requirements.
  Example: requires Predicate<T> Default: Preserve.
- RequiresExpressionSpaceBeforeBrace: Space before a requires-expression
  body.
  Example: requires(T t) { Default: Preserve.
- CompoundRequirementInteriorSpacing: Spaces inside compound-requirement
  expression braces.
  Example: { value() } noexcept; Default: GeneratedSpace.
- TypeConstraintSpaceBefore: Space before a compound requirement's
  constraint arrow.
  Example: { value() } -> Concept; Default: GeneratedSpace.
- TypeConstraintSpaceAfter: Space after a compound requirement's constraint
  arrow.
  Example: { value() } -> Concept; Default: GeneratedSpace.
- FunctionTryBlockSpacing: Space before a function try-block introducer.
  Example: void F() try {} Default: GeneratedSpace.
- RecordFinalSpacing: Space before a parsed class final specifier.
  Example: struct Widget final Default: GeneratedSpace.
- AlignasSpaceAfterSpecifier: Space after a complete alignas specifier.
  Example: alignas(16) int value; Default: GeneratedSpace.
- LeadingScopeResolutionSpacing: Space before leading global ::, not between
  qualifiers.
  Example: const ::Detail::Type Default: Preserve.
- ControlHeaderSemicolonSpaceBefore: Space before a control-header
  semicolon.
  Example: for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i) Default: GeneratedNoSpace.
- StatementSequenceSpacing: Horizontal space between complete sibling
  statements/declarations.
  Example: first(); second(); Default: GeneratedSpace.
- BlockCommentSpacing: Spaces beside ordinary block comments; argument rules
  win.
  Example: value /* note */ Default: Preserve.
- ControlClauseSpacing: Spaces before else/catch/do-while suffixes and in
  else if.
  Example: if (x) {} else if (y) {} Default: GeneratedSpace.
- ControlBodySpacing: Space before a controlled statement; brace-specific
  rules win.
  Example: if (x) Use(); Default: Preserve.
- NoexceptSpecifierInteriorSpacing: Spaces inside a noexcept specifier's
  outer parentheses.
  Example: noexcept(test) Default: Preserve.
- OperatorNameInteriorSpacing: Spaces inside an operator() or operator[]
  name.
  Example: operator() Default: Preserve.
- DeleteArrayInteriorSpacing: Space inside delete-array brackets.
  Example: delete [] pointer; Default: Preserve.
- StructuredBindingSpaceBeforeBrackets: Space before a structured binding's
  opening bracket.
  Example: auto [first, second] = value; Default: Preserve.
- MemberPointerSpaceBeforeQualifier: Space before a member-pointer's owner
  qualifier.
  Example: int Owner::*member; Default: Preserve.
- NestedDeclaratorSpaceBeforeParentheses: Space before nested-declarator
  parentheses.
  Example: int (*callback)(int); Default: Preserve.
- PointerQualifierSpacing: Spaces between consecutive pointer cv-qualifiers.
  Example: int* const volatile pointer; Default: Preserve.
- StringLiteralFragmentSpacing: Space between adjacent string-literal
  fragments.
  Example: "left" "right" Default: GeneratedSpace.
- AsmKeywordSpacing: Spaces between a parsed asm introducer and its
  modifiers.
  Example: asm volatile ("nop"); Default: Preserve.
- AsmSpaceBeforeParentheses: Space before an asm declaration's operand
  parentheses.
  Example: asm ("nop"); Default: Preserve.
- AsmInteriorSpacing: Spaces inside an asm declaration's outer parentheses.
  Example: asm ( "nop" ); Default: Preserve.
- ControlKeywordModifierSpacing: Spaces before constexpr/consteval/co_await
  control modifiers.
  Example: if constexpr (ready) {} Default: Preserve.
- DependentTemplateNameSpacing: Space after template in a confirmed
  dependent name.
  Example: object.template Method<int>(); Default: Preserve.
- DependentTypeNameSpacing: Space after typename in a confirmed dependent
  type name.
  Example: typename T::type value; Default: Preserve.
  All accept the five spacing policies above. Preserve retains horizontal
  spelling independently of layout. Specific rules, lexical separation
  and protected/opaque syntax take priority.

Common spacing
    DeclarationSpecifierSpacing = Preserve
    DeclarationNameSpacing = Space
    # Keeps const   int, but normalizes int   value to int value.
    FunctionSpaceBeforeParameters = NoSpace
    ControlBlockKeywordSpacing = Space
    CommaSpaceBefore = NoSpace
    CommaSpaceAfter = Space
    EmptyBlockInteriorSpacing = GeneratedNoSpace
    ScopeResolutionSpacing = NoSpace
    TemplateTypeArgumentInteriorSpacing = NoSpace

Comments, attributes and qualifiers
    ArgumentCommentSpacing = Space
    ArgumentNameCommentSpacing = NoSpace
    # Example: consume(/* note */ value, /*count=*/size /* suffix */);
    AttributeSpaceBeforeSpecifier = Space
    AttributeSpaceAfterSpecifier = Space
    FunctionRefQualifierSpacing = Space
    FunctionCvRefQualifierSpacing = NoSpace

Call annotations
- CallLineMessageNames keeps preceding arguments and the first complete
  string literal beside the opener, then puts each following format argument
  on its own line even if the call fits. Default: []. Exact unqualified
  names, including members; overrides both first-argument pin lists.
  CallLineMessageOverflow controls width conflicts: Wrap (default) wraps
  headers at argument boundaries before splitting nested expressions.
  Concatenated literals may wrap after a fitting first fragment beside the
  opener. KeepInline keeps the header through the complete message on the
  call line, even past ColumnLimit. Required nested breaks
  still win. Calls without a complete
  literal use ordinary layout. Preserve/Always and protection still win.
- CallLineFirstArgumentNames pins any first argument beside the opener;
  CallLineFirstStringNames pins string literals only. Both default to [].
  Use exact unqualified call names, including members; the general list
  wins overlaps. Overflow: Wrap normally; BreakBefore moves the argument
  below the opener if it cannot fit in full; KeepInline retains its opening
  piece even past ColumnLimit. Nested syntax may wrap; literals split only
  between pieces. Preserve/Always win.
- ChainedCallRootNames: [] selects all safe chains; otherwise use listed
  roots. AlwaysBreakChainRootNames may break even one following call.
  Omit template arguments and the template keyword:
  ns::Builder<T>::Make<U> matches ns::Builder::Make, not Make.
- AlwaysBreakChainCallNames starts matching member calls on a new line,
  including in control headers. Use exact unqualified names without template
  arguments or the template keyword. Fields and root calls do not match.
  Obeys ChainsLayout/root selection; overrides sticky pairs at matching calls.
  Safety and protected regions still win.
- IndentedCallScopePairs preserves extra indentation between matched calls
  in one context and label section. GenerateCallScopeIndentation creates it
  from flat input; requires non-Preserve IndentationKind.
- IndentedChainScopePairs indents inside matched chain calls;
  StickyChainCallPairs keeps fitting consecutive calls together.
  Both obey ChainsLayout/root selection; sticky pairs may name a factory.
- Pairs use exact First:Second names, not patterns. Scope endpoints must
  be distinct and unambiguous; sticky pairs may share names.

Message and format arguments
    CallLineMessageNames = ["LOG_MESSAGE", "RAISE_ERROR"]
    CallLineMessageOverflow = Wrap
    # LOG_MESSAGE(code, "Value: %v, count: %v",
    #     value,
    #     count);

Pin first arguments
    CallLineFirstArgumentNames = ["dispatch"]
    CallLineFirstArgumentOverflow = Wrap
    CallLineFirstStringNames = ["WRITE_MESSAGE"]
    CallLineFirstStringOverflow = BreakBefore

Chain roots and forced breaks
    ChainedCallRootNames = ["factory", "pipeline::Builder"]
    AlwaysBreakChainRootNames = ["WRITE_EVENT", "logging::Event"]
    # Root names omit template arguments: factory<T> matches factory.
    AlwaysBreakChainCallNames = ["Then", "Finish"]
    # Break before .Then(), ->Finish(), or .Then<T>(), even when they fit.

Paired calls and chains
    IndentationKind = Spaces
    IndentedCallScopePairs = ["OpenItems:CloseItems"]
    GenerateCallScopeIndentation = true
    IndentedChainScopePairs = ["BeginMap:EndMap", "BeginList:EndList"]
    StickyChainCallPairs = ["Item:Value", "registrar:Parameter"]

Include directives
- IncludeDelimiters: Preserve (default) or QuotedLocal. QuotedLocal uses
  quotes for explicit ./ and ../ paths and lexically same-directory paths,
  and angles for other paths using IncludePathBase and the input filename;
  it never changes paths.
  Bare names retain their delimiters: spelling alone cannot distinguish
  local headers from system headers such as <stdio.h> or <vector>.
  For example, <../sibling/header.h> becomes "../sibling/header.h".
  This policy works independently of sorting, including commented and
  standalone includes. Macros, splices and protected directives stay exact.
- IncludeOrder = Sort sorts safe blocks;
  Preserve disables sorting. IncludeMatchingHeader = First pairs a
  source with a same-directory, same-stem .h/.hh/.hpp/.hxx/.h++ header.
  Header inputs or missing source filenames disable pairing.
- IncludeMatchingHeaderSuffix: a literal, case-sensitive suffix removed once
  from the source stem for additional matches; exact-stem matches still pair.
  Default "" disables removal; an empty remaining stem never pairs.
  For example, "_test" also pairs example_test.cpp with example.hpp in the
  same directory when IncludeMatchingHeader = First. Header inputs, angled
  includes and headers in other directories remain unpaired.
- IncludeCategoryOrder: QuotedFirst (default) or AngledFirst orders quoted
  and angled headers in every grouping mode. Matching headers stay first
  and IncludeStandardHeaders = Last keeps unclaimed standard headers last.
- IncludeSortPriority: CategoryFirst (default) sorts quote/angle categories
  before semantic groups. GroupFirst applies priorities, IncludeGroupOrder
  and directory subgroup ordering before IncludeCategoryOrder. Mixed
  delimiters remain separate subgroups within each semantic group.
  Matching headers stay first and unclaimed standard headers stay last.
  IncludeGrouping = Preserve ignores this policy. Delimiters are unchanged.
- IncludeGrouping: Preserve keeps adjacent blocks and blank lines;
  Regroup merges across blanks by rule; Directory groups by directory.
  Regroup/Directory insert one blank line, conflicting with MaxEmptyLines
  = 0. Preserve ignores group rules; Regroup ignores Depth.
- IncludeGroups uses Prefix or Prefixes, StandardHeaders = true, or both,
  with Depth (default 127) and Priority (default 0). First matching rule wins.
  Entries ending in / match directory prefixes; other nonempty entries
  match exact header names, quoted or angled. Entries must be unique,
  normalized relative paths; a sole empty Prefix is a fallback.
  Depth: 0 keeps a rule together; 1..126 limits suffix directory depth;
  127 keeps full directories. Aliases merge groups without rewriting paths.
  Exact-name aliases use their containing directories for grouping.
- StandardHeaders = true selects angled names with neither / nor .h,
  using the same lexical heuristic as IncludeStandardHeaders = Last.
  Prefix or Prefixes adds headers to that group, for example
  {StandardHeaders = true, Prefixes = ["stdio.h", "stdint.h"]}.
  A matched standard group overrides Last and uses ordinary group ordering;
  it stays alphabetical and ignores Depth. At most one such rule is allowed.
  Put it before an empty-prefix fallback, which otherwise claims its headers.
- Priority: -128..127, lower first; within quoted/angled categories under
  CategoryFirst, or across them under GroupFirst.
  IncludeGroupOrder: Directory or Configured (rule order); unmatched
  groups use priority 0 and follow matched groups in a configured tier.
  Priority affects output order, not matching. Rules validate even if inactive.
- IncludeDirectoryOrder and IncludeInGroupOrder choose NearestFirst or
  Alphabetical. Distance counts parent/child steps from the input directory;
  ties are alphabetical. In-group distance ignores Depth and aliases.
  Paired and standard-header groups stay alphabetical.
- IncludePathBase: SourceDirectory or ProjectRoot (working directory).
  Bare quoted names and quoted ./ or ../ paths stay source-relative;
  absolute paths stay absolute. Paths are lexical; no filesystem lookup.
- IncludeStandardHeaders = Last adds a final alphabetical group for angled
  names with neither / nor .h, unless matched by a StandardHeaders rule.
  This includes extensionless vendor names; quoted names and stdio.h do not
  qualify automatically. Preserve applies ordinary category and group sorting.
- Comments, macros, conditionals, protection and mixed line endings stop
  blocks. Attached-comment blocks stay exact; hunks must cover a whole
  block. Check mode reports one include-order failure per file.

Group headers by directory
    IncludeOrder = Sort
    IncludeCategoryOrder = AngledFirst
    IncludeMatchingHeader = First
    IncludeGrouping = Directory
    IncludeGroupOrder = Configured
    IncludeDirectoryOrder = NearestFirst
    IncludeInGroupOrder = Alphabetical
    IncludePathBase = SourceDirectory
    IncludeStandardHeaders = Last
    IncludeGroups = [
        { Prefix = "library/", Depth = 1 },
        { Prefix = "util/", Depth = 1 }
    ]
    # library/core/a.hpp and library/core/detail/b.hpp share a group.

Order semantic groups before quote/angle categories
    IncludeOrder = Sort
    IncludeGrouping = Regroup
    IncludeSortPriority = GroupFirst
    IncludeCategoryOrder = AngledFirst
    IncludeGroupOrder = Configured
    IncludeGroups = [
        { Prefix = "vendor/", Priority = 1 },
        { Prefix = "framework/", Priority = 2 },
        { Prefix = "shared/", Priority = 3 },
        { Prefix = "app/", Priority = 4 }
    ]
    # <vendor/api.hpp>, "framework/api.hpp", <shared/api.hpp>, <app/api.hpp>
    # appear in that order, with one blank line between groups.

Put standard headers before unknown projects and system headers
    Layout = Canonical
    IncludeOrder = Sort
    IncludeGrouping = Directory
    IncludeGroups = [
        { Prefix = "project/", Priority = 0 },
        { StandardHeaders = true, Prefixes = ["stdio.h", "stdint.h"],
          Priority = 1 },
        { Prefix = "", Priority = 2 }
    ]
    # Project headers precede standard headers, then system headers.
    # <stdio.h> and <vector> precede <unknown_project/header.h> and <Windows.h>.
    # To put standard headers first, give their rule Priority = -1.

Order project groups first
    IncludeOrder = Sort
    IncludeGrouping = Directory
    IncludeGroupOrder = Directory
    IncludeDirectoryOrder = NearestFirst
    IncludeInGroupOrder = NearestFirst
    IncludePathBase = ProjectRoot
    IncludeGroups = [
        { Prefix = "library/", Priority = 1 },
        { Prefix = "util/", Priority = 2 },
        { Prefix = "", Depth = 3, Priority = 0 }
    ]
    # ProjectRoot uses the working directory for input filenames.

Templates and comments
- BreakAfterTemplateHeader requires canonical DeclarationHeadersLayout.
- RequiresClausePosition also requires canonical DeclarationHeadersLayout.
  Preserve (default) keeps placement. OwnLine separates the clause;
  OwnLineWithBrace allows an attached function brace. WithPreceding prefers
  the preceding header; WithFollowing prefers the following declaration or
  brace. SingleLine prefers both sides inline, wrapping when necessary.
  Explicit brace placement and protected/comment boundaries take precedence.
  A prefix clause's position overrides BreakAfterTemplateHeader. SingleLine
  also joins the template header of a function with a trailing clause.
- IndentRequiresClause (default true) adds IndentWidth for OwnLine,
  OwnLineWithBrace and WithFollowing. Other explicit positions use the
  ordinary continuation offset when wrapped. Requires-expressions and
  nested requirements are unaffected.
- StandaloneCommentBlankLinesBefore/After uses blank-line counts and caps.
- RulerLength: 0 preserves standalone all-slash comments; 3..10000 sets
  the slash count. RulerBlankLinesBefore/After overrides surrounding blanks.
- RulerPlacement: Preserve (default) keeps placement. NamespaceBoundaries adds
  rulers inside namespace openings and before closings. Initial using namespace
  directives stay with the opening. Existing ruler placement is preserved,
  including between declarations and inside records or functions. Protected
  bytes and directive boundaries are respected. New rulers use RulerLength,
  or 80 when it is 0. Inter-declaration grouping is left to the author.

Template headers and comments
    DeclarationHeadersLayout = Canonical
    BreakAfterTemplateHeader = true
    StandaloneCommentBlankLinesBefore = Preserve
    StandaloneCommentBlankLinesAfter = Preserve
    RulerLength = 0
    RulerPlacement = Preserve
    RulerBlankLinesBefore = Preserve
    RulerBlankLinesAfter = Preserve

Put constraints on an indented line
    DeclarationHeadersLayout = Canonical
    RequiresClausePosition = OwnLine
    IndentRequiresClause = true
    # Result:
    # template<class T>
    #     requires Ready<T>
    # void process(T value);

Safety and checks
- hit-fmt off/on always protects text. HonorClangFormatDirectives
  defaults to true for clang-format off/on.
- CallLikeMacroNames opts exact, unqualified standalone macro invocations
  into C++ call formatting, including lambda arguments. A trailing semicolon
  is optional. Use it only when arguments follow C++ expression grammar;
  it does not expand macros or infer declarations. Calls use Calls options.
  Other macro syntax hints for the same name are rejected. Unlisted macros
  keep their existing handling. WhitespaceSensitiveMacros and
  off/on directives still protect invocation bytes.
- FunctionBodyMacroNames marks balanced invocations followed by function
  bodies; its arguments stay opaque rather than becoming call arguments.
- ClassMacroNames uses Begin:End pairs of unqualified function-like macros.
  Endpoints must be distinct and unique; nested pairs must match.
- EnumMacroNames formats YT-style enum sequences in the final argument.
  Entries may be (Name) or ((Name)(value)). Each gets an indented line;
  value fields align with at least one space, and the closer gets its
  own line. Optional header arguments give the enum name and underlying
  type. Headers stay inline; value expressions keep their internal
  spelling. Unknown forms and explicit protection stay intact.
- Brace insertion, null statement/declaration removal and namespace-comment
  changes are opt-in. Required semicolons, uncertain macro syntax and
  commented placeholders stay; no transform crosses protection.
- IndentPreprocessingDirectives accepts Preserve (default), Indent and
  NoIndent. Preserve keeps directive indentation and space after # or %:.
  Indent moves whole directives by conditional depth using IndentWidth;
  else/elif/endif align with their if. NoIndent puts directives at column
  one. Indent and NoIndent remove space after # or %:. Legacy true means
  Indent; false means Preserve. AlignMacroBackslashes aligns existing
  continuations after the longest prefix; tokens/spliced text stay intact.
- Naming/parameter-name checks report issues without renaming code.
  Exclude filters paths; NamingNamespaceExemptions exempts namespaces.
  Presets approximate style guides, not clang-format compatibility.

Macro hints
    HonorClangFormatDirectives = true
    CallLikeMacroNames = ["REGISTER_HANDLER"]
    FunctionBodyMacroNames = ["DEFINE_CASE", "DEFINE_METHOD"]
    EnumMacroNames = ["DEFINE_ENUM", "DEFINE_BIT_ENUM"]
    ClassMacroNames = ["BEGIN_SETTER:END_SETTER"]
    WhitespaceSensitiveMacros = ["STRINGIZE_TEXT"]

    # With EnumMacroNames enabled:
    # DEFINE_ENUM(EPhase,
    #     ((Waiting) (0))
    #     ((Running) (1))
    # );

Optional transforms
    AddMissingControlBraces = false
    RemoveNullStatements = false
    RemoveNullDeclarations = false
    IncludeOrder = Preserve
    IncludeDelimiters = Preserve
    NamespaceComments = Preserve
    IndentPreprocessingDirectives = Preserve
    AlignMacroBackslashes = false

NamespaceComments Fix and Remove rewrite only namespace closing labels,
such as `// namespace Name` or a bare matching namespace name.
Fix also merges `// namespace` with an immediately following line comment
containing only the exact namespace name. Blank gaps and protected comments
prevent merging.
Explanatory comments stay intact. With Fix or AddMissing, a missing closing
label is still reported when an explanatory comment prevents a safe fix.
ShortNamespaceLines exemptions apply to missing labels.

Naming checks and excluded paths
    FunctionDeclarationParameterNames = Required
    FunctionDefinitionParameterNames = NameOrCommentedNameRequired
    ClassNameCase = PascalCase
    ClassNamePrefixes = []
    NamingStandardLibraryExemptions = true
    NamingExemptions = ["external_name"]
    FunctionNameExemptions = ["custom_begin", "custom_end"]
    NamingNamespaceExemptions = ["std"]
    Exclude = ["(^|/)generated(/|$)"]

Naming rules only report diagnostics; --fix never renames identifiers.
Each category has NameCase, NamePrefixes, NameSuffixes and NameExemptions.
NameCase accepts Inherit (unspecified), Unchecked, camelCase, PascalCase,
snake_case and SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE. Affix [] disables that check; [""]
permits no affix. Case checks use the name between matching affixes.
Exemptions are exact, case-sensitive, unqualified identifier names.
NamingExemptions applies to all categories, in addition to each category's
NameExemptions. Lists replace inherited lists; [] clears custom exemptions.
NamingStandardLibraryExemptions defaults to false. The YTsaurus preset
enables it. When true, the entry-point name main and function names begin,
end, cbegin, cend, rbegin, rend, crbegin, crend, size, ssize, empty, data,
swap, get, iter_move, iter_swap, what, lock, unlock, try_lock, try_lock_for,
try_lock_until, lock_shared, unlock_shared, try_lock_shared,
try_lock_shared_for and try_lock_shared_until bypass case, affix and acronym
checks.
This covers member and free function definitions by name;
signatures and inheritance are not verified. Other categories and
parameter-name presence
checks are unaffected. Custom exemptions work with either setting.

The exception and mutex names support standard exception overrides and locking
protocols. Project-specific names such as hash_value require custom exemptions.

Variable roles, in precedence order:
- Parameter falls back to Variable; GlobalVariable falls back to Variable.
- StaticLocalVariable falls back to LocalVariable, then Variable.
- StaticMemberVariable falls back to PublicMemberVariable,
  ProtectedMemberVariable or PrivateMemberVariable, then MemberVariable,
  then Variable. Non-static members start at their access-specific rule.
- A non-Inherit case, nonempty affix list or exemption list selects the
  whole rule. Its fields replace the fallback; Unchecked can disable it.
- Static locals use their storage role even inside methods. Local classes
  use member rules. Struct/union members default public; class members
  default private. Anonymous unions use their enclosing scope and access.
  Macro-generated records need explicit access labels
  for access-specific rules, otherwise they use MemberVariable.
- Local const/constexpr variables retain their local role. Structured
  bindings use their declaration's variable rule.
- Access labels flow through preprocessing branches. If branches disagree,
  subsequent fields use general member rules until access is explicit.

Parameter rules check named parameters in definitions and declarations.
Function names and forward type names remain definition-only checks.
Enumerator rules cover native enumerators and entries of EnumMacroNames;
Enum rules also cover the declared type name in these macros.

    ClassNamePrefixes = ["T", "I", "E"]
    LocalVariableNameCase = camelCase
    LocalVariableNameExemptions = ["Logger", "_", "this_"]
    ParameterNameExemptions = ["Logger", "_", "this_"]
    ParameterNameCase = camelCase
    GlobalVariableNameCase = PascalCase
    StaticLocalVariableNameCase = PascalCase
    PublicMemberVariableNameCase = PascalCase
    PrivateMemberVariableNameCase = PascalCase
    PrivateMemberVariableNameSuffixes = ["_"]
    StaticMemberVariableNameCase = PascalCase
    EnumeratorNameCase = PascalCase
    NamingAcronyms = ["IO", "IP", "TCP", "MPSC"]

NamingAcronyms accepts uppercase words of at least two letters; [] disables
acronym checks. With camelCase/PascalCase, known two-letter words stay
uppercase, longer words are capitalized, and initial camelCase words stay
lowercase: TIOBuffer, TTcpBuffer, ioBuffer, tcpBuffer. Configured affixes
are stripped first. Matching uses complete camel-case words ignoring case,
with digits as boundaries; unknown words and ambiguous boundaries remain
unchecked. This vocabulary does not identify abbreviations automatically.
Choose unambiguous vocabulary: CH may mean ClickHouse or character, so it is
omitted from the YTsaurus preset. Its Logger exemption also covers parameters
and non-public fields; _ and this_ are exempt locals and parameters.
